van't Hoff Factor (i) = \frac{\text{Observed freezing point}}{\text{Calculated freezing point}} = \frac{0.0205 \text{ K}}{0.0197 \text{ K}} = 1.041
Acetic acid is a weak electrolyte and will dissociate into two ions: acetate and hydrogen ions per molecule of acetic acid. If x is the degree of dissociation of acetic acid, then we would have n(1-x) moles of undissociated acetic acid, nx moles of CH_3COO^- and nx moles of H^+ ions,
\begin{array}{ccc} \mathrm{CH_3COOH} \rightleftharpoons & \mathrm{H^+ + CH_3COO^-} \\ n \, \mathrm{mol} & 0 & 0 \\ n \, (1 - x) & nx \, \mathrm{mol} & nx \, \mathrm{mol} \end{array}
Thus total moles of particles are: n(1 - x + x + x) = n(1 + x)
i = \frac{n(1+x)}{n} = 1+x = 1.041
Thus degree of dissociation of acetic acid = x = 1.041 - 1.000 = 0.041
Then [CH_3COOH] = n(1-x) = 0.0106 (1-0.041),
[CH_3COO^-] = nx = 0.0106 \times 0.041, [H^+] = nx = 0.0106 \times 0.041.
K_{a} = \frac{[CH_{3}COO^{-}][H^{+}]}{[CH_{3}COOH]} = \frac{0.0106 \times 0.041 \times 0.0106 \times 0.041}{0.0106 (1.00 - 0.041)}= 1.86 \times 10^{-5}
